# General Rules

## Project Context
This repository contains a media center application with:
- a Go backend
- a React frontend

The product is focused on local media management and playback.  
Prefer solutions that support performance, maintainability, accessibility, and a clean user experience.

## General Expectations
- Follow the existing architecture and patterns already present in the codebase.
- Prefer incremental improvements over broad rewrites.
- Keep solutions practical and production-oriented.
- Avoid unnecessary abstraction.
- Do not introduce new dependencies unless there is a clear and justified need.
- Reuse existing utilities, helpers, components, and patterns before creating new ones.
- When making changes, keep them scoped to the task being worked on.

## Code Style
- Write clear, readable, and direct code.
- Prefer simple control flow over clever or overly compact solutions.
- Use descriptive names for variables, functions, types, and components.
- Keep functions and components focused on a single responsibility.
- Avoid deeply nested logic when a clearer structure is possible.
- Prefer explicit code over magic behavior.
- Avoid premature optimization.

## Comments
- Avoid comments unless they are strictly necessary.
- Do not add comments that simply restate what the code already makes obvious.
- Only use comments when they provide important context, explain a non-obvious constraint, document a workaround, or clarify a decision that cannot be easily inferred from the code itself.

## Backend Rules
- Use Go idioms and standard library patterns whenever reasonable.
- Keep handlers thin and push business logic into appropriate services or application layers.
- Validate input at the boundary of the system.
- Return consistent and predictable error responses.
- Handle errors explicitly and avoid swallowing them.
- Prefer straightforward database and file handling logic.
- Be careful with file streaming, range requests, and large media handling.
- Prioritize reliability and efficiency for local media serving.
- Do not refactor working backend code unless the task requires it.
- All constants go in the file constants.go
- All helper or utilities go in the hlpers package
- Never define types inside of functions
- All functions, variables or types in the main package should start with a capital letter.

## Go Error Handling
- Never use the `if x, err := ...; err != nil {}` pattern.
- Always assign the returned values first.
- Always place `if err != nil {}` on its own line immediately after the assignment when checking errors.
- This rule applies to all Go code in the repository.

Preferred:

```go
info, err := os.Stat(path)
if err != nil {
    return err
}
Not allowed:
if info, err := os.Stat(path); err != nil {
    return err
}

## Frontend Rules
- Use React and TypeScript patterns consistent with the existing application.
- Always use functional components.
- Do not use memoization hooks because we are using the react compiler.
- Keep components small, focused, and composable.
- Reuse existing UI components before creating new ones.
- Keep styling consistent with the existing design system and theme.
- Prefer clear state flows and predictable component behavior.
- Avoid unnecessary client-side complexity.
- Prefer accessible markup and keyboard-friendly interactions where relevant.
- Helper and utilities go in the lib directory.
- All constants must always go in the constants.ts file inside of the lib directory.
- Components should always go directly in the components directory.

## Data Fetching and State
- Follow the existing project conventions for data fetching and state management.
- Use the established libraries and patterns already used in the application.
- Keep server state and UI state clearly separated.
- Avoid duplicated fetching logic when reusable hooks or utilities already exist.
- Do not introduce global state unless there is a clear need.

## Performance
- Prefer efficient solutions, but do not sacrifice readability without a clear reason.
- Be mindful of rendering costs on the frontend.
- Be mindful of I/O, streaming behavior, and memory usage on the backend.
- Optimize only where there is an actual need or a known bottleneck.
- Do not add memoization, caching, or complex optimizations by default.

## Accessibility and UX
- Keep accessibility in mind when building UI.
- Prefer semantic HTML where possible.
- Ensure interactive elements are understandable and usable.
- Avoid UI patterns that make navigation harder.
- Keep the interface clean and consistent.

## Media Center Product Direction
- Keep the project focused on local media playback and management.
- Do not introduce features that push the product toward unrelated scope such as plugin ecosystems, torrent integrations, or unnecessary platform-style complexity unless explicitly requested.
- Prefer solutions that improve the core experience of browsing, managing, and playing local media.

## When Making Changes
- First understand the existing implementation.
- Match the style and structure of nearby code.
- Change only what is needed for the task.
- Do not perform unrelated cleanup unless it is required to complete the work safely.
- Preserve backwards compatibility unless the task explicitly calls for a breaking change.

## Output Expectations
- Produce code that is ready to integrate.
- Do not include unnecessary explanatory text inside the code.
- Keep diffs clean and focused.
- When relevant, mention important tradeoffs, assumptions, or follow-up work briefly and clearly.
